  "query_preview": "..\n\nSynoptic Setup: \n\nA band of reflectivity has shown up overnight across southern tier\nzones, but is mostly virga as no recent observations have\nindicated measurable precipitation. Have introduced a band of\nsilent or in some cases slight chance pop into Friday lifting\nnorth with a weak warm front.\n\nThe next shortwave will move in Friday night into Saturday as\nnorthwest flow aloft gets established over NE Montana. Look for\nsomewhat cooler weather on Saturday. A stray shower is possible\nbut most should remain dry.\n\nEnsembles show upper ridging moving in from the west into early\nnext week. This will lead to warming temperatures and highs in the\nafternoon returning into the 70s and 80s. The warm up will be\nshort lived however with the next trough approaching for Tuesday\nwith highs a few degrees lower.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNBM was largely considered for the forecast cycle, though\ndeviations were made in some instances. For one, Friday morning\nlows were reduced a few degrees in places across northeast zones\nwhere the Thursday night drop was running ahead of schedule.\n\nOtherwise, pops were also adjusted upward given the radar\npresentation and weak front. Trends will need to be monitored for\nadditional tweaks, several model guidance solutions such as the\nNAM Nest and various high resolution models lend support to\nisolated showers. Little if any QPF is expected to result from\nthese low confidence showers.",
